  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or checked that the rule's query matches any documents.\n\n**Data:** The logs data view (`timestamp` is the time field) holds web requests. The fields I used:\n- `response`: the status code, stored as a string, so I match it through `response.keyword`.\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src`\n- `clientip`\n- `agent`\n- `machine.os`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-599ed815 Web logs overview\" has five panels and a default range of the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Average bytes by extension\n- Top source countries\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-599ed815-web-logs-overview-b782f0b65c2a.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-599ed815 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- It runs every minute and looks at the last 5 minutes of `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It fires when the number of documents with `response.keyword` matching `5[0-9][0-9]` is above 10.\n- I created it without any actions, so it will raise alerts in Kibana but won't notify anyone.",
